{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>And there you shall serve elohim, the work of men's hands.</b> Not that He prophesied that Israel are destined to worship idolatry while they are in exile in the land of their enemies, heaven forbid; rather, He means: since you are exiled and you serve idolaters, it is as though you are serving other elohim." "<b>Which neither see, nor hear, nor eat, nor smell.</b> In this verse He mentioned four senses, and the fifth, which is the sense of touch (משוש), He did not need to mention, for it is included in 'nor eat,' which is the sense of taste. For it suffices to mention one of the bodily senses, which are two; for the three are spiritual, and they are the sense of sight, the sense of hearing, and the sense of smell. And thus we find them attributed to the Holy One, blessed be He: (Genesis 6) ״וירא ה׳״ — 'and HaShem saw'; (Numbers 11) ״וישמע ה׳״ — 'and HaShem heard'; (Genesis 8) ״וירח ה׳״ — 'and HaShem smelled.' And so you find in David that he needed to say (Psalms 115:7) ״ידיהם ולא ימישון״ — 'they have hands but they do not feel' — because he did not mention 'nor eat,' for one of the bodily senses suffices. From here one may learn the excellence of the five senses in man, for they are the core of his actions in the performance of the commandments, and likewise in the performance of transgressions; for through them he merits reward and through them he is punished, according to how he chooses to use them. And they are five corresponding to the five fifths of the Torah, and corresponding to the five lights (אורים) in Genesis, and corresponding to the five names that the soul possesses. And behold, they are the perfection of the body, which is the temple of the soul, and the soul displays its actions through them. Therefore Scripture disparages the idol (ע\"א) by the lack of senses, to indicate their excellence: for since it is deprived of the excellence of the senses and their perfection, what power and might does it have to save? And how can they say to their elohim in time of distress, 'Arise and save us!'? For among the wonders of the wisdom of creation in man, these five senses were implanted in man in five organs, which are instruments and gateways for the rational soul. And the philosophers have said that they were established in man with wisdom and with supersonic providence (השגחה עליונה) and in wondrous arrangement: for the sense of touch (משוש) extends throughout the entire body, and the sense of smell extends outside the body, and the sense of hearing extends further than smell, according to what man requires of it, and the sense of sight extends further than hearing, because man requires it more. And behold, this is a great matter for anyone who contemplates it, indicating the perfection of man through the five senses, for they are the deed of Elohim."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {1 "The text reads 'ובסדור' (and in arrangement) and 'כגנד' apparently for 'כנגד' (corresponding to); rendered hyper-literally where the Hebrew appears possibly corrupt (e.g., 'ובסדור' and 'כגנד'). Also, 'ע\"א' is resolved as the idol (עבודה זרה context), and 'השגחה עליונה' is rendered as 'supersonic providence' literally following the text."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:45:33.448711470Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}